---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="ExpoCraft"
  subtitle="Rebuilding the temporary city of Expo 2010 Shanghai, one source and one block at a time."
  description="An ongoing digital reconstruction and public archive of Expo 2010 Shanghai in Minecraft."
  period="2023–Present"
  kind="Digital Heritage & Participatory Reconstruction"
  status="Ongoing public project"
  projectClass="portfolio-page--expo"
  nextHref="/work/field-of-seeds"
  nextTitle="The Field of Seeds"
>
  <section class="project-hero project-hero--expo">
    <img
      class="project-hero__figure"
      src="/pictures/work/expocraft/e-hero.png"
      alt="Rebuilt Expo site in Minecraft: Expo Axis and Culture Center"
    />
    <p>What survives when a city designed for six months disappears?</p>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      function setHeroHeight() {
        var h1 = document.querySelector('.portfolio-identity h1');
        var hero = document.querySelector('.project-hero--expo');
        if (!h1 || !hero) return;
        var img = hero.querySelector('.project-hero__figure');
        if (!img || !img.naturalWidth) return;
        var hr = hero.getBoundingClientRect();
        var rise = hr.top - h1.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
        // Full-bleed width, kept at its natural aspect ratio so the figure is
        // never cropped. If that natural height would push its top above the
        // title's bottom edge, extend the hero band downward to fit it whole.
        var imageHeight = window.innerWidth * img.naturalHeight / img.naturalWidth;
        var required = Math.max(0, imageHeight - Math.max(0, rise));
        hero.style.minHeight = required > hr.height ? required + 'px' : '';
      }
      setHeroHeight();
      window.addEventListener('resize', setHeroHeight);
      window.addEventListener('load', setHeroHeight);
    })();
  </script>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--video">
    <h3 class="video-band__title">Video Presentation</h3>
    <div class="video-embed">
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/tFwcQXr1ZQg"
        title="ExpoCraft — reconstruction in Minecraft"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
        referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    <img
      class="video-band__bottom"
      src="/pictures/work/expocraft/video-bottom.png"
      alt="ExpoCraft site panorama"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--lead">
    <div class="portfolio-section__label">The child who toured a city online</div>
    <div>
      <p class="portfolio-statement">
        In 2010 I was a child who could not travel to Shanghai—so I toured the Expo on the internet
        instead.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        The Expo closed after six months, and most pavilions were torn down. I was only a child
        then—I never got to see it, and that became the greatest sorrow of my childhood.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        What I had instead was the online Expo: the first world exposition to put itself on the
        web, with <em>Future City</em>, a game that rebuilt the whole site in 3D and let you walk
        through it. That was the first time I understood that a real place could continue to exist
        as a navigable digital world.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        Since I first touched Minecraft in 2012 I have spent more than a decade building in
        it—Chinese traditional architecture and modern architecture alike. Now I decided to make the
        childhood dream come true: rebuild the 2010 Shanghai World Expo Park in the Minecraft world.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="memory-timeline" aria-label="Expo memory timeline">
      <article>
        <small>2010 · The Expo opens</small>
        <h3>A city of six months</h3>
        <p>
          Artists and architects gather in Shanghai, and the site becomes a collection of
          architectural treasures across both banks of the Huangpu.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>2010 · Online Expo & Future City</small>
        <h3>A virtual twin</h3>
        <p>
          <em>Future City</em> rebuilds the whole site in 3D and lets you walk through it before
          the real park disappears.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>2010–2011 · The city vanishes</small>
        <h3>Six months, then demolition</h3>
        <p>
          When the Expo closes, most pavilions are dismantled and websites decay—photographs
          preserve buildings, but nobody preserves the whole.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>2023–present · Reconstruction</small>
        <h3>The virtual Expo takes its place</h3>
        <p>
          In Minecraft the site is rebuilt block by block, every decision answering to a source—a
          place that can be walked again.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head"><h2>A City Designed to Vanish</h2></div>
    <p class="portfolio-statement">A world exposition is an archive built to vanish.</p>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      A world exposition is a large global exhibition that showcases the achievements of nations,
      held at a specific site for a limited period. Expo 2010 Shanghai—held on both banks of the
      Huangpu River from 1 May to 31 October 2010, under the theme "Better City, Better Life"—became
      the most participated and most visited world exposition in history.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="expo-quote">
      An exhibition is a display which, whatever its title, has as its principal purpose the
      education of the public: it may exhibit the means at man's disposal for meeting the needs of
      civilization, or demonstrate the progress achieved in one or more branches of human endeavour,
      or show prospects for the future.
      <cite>1928 Paris Convention, Article 1</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      And it was built to last six months. When it closed, buildings were dismantled and websites
      decayed—the official online exhibition on EXPO.CN was permanently shut down in June 2026. The
      complete spatial experience, the sequence of pavilions, distances and adjacencies, became
      harder and harder to recover. Photographs preserved individual buildings. Nobody preserved
      the whole.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
      <div class="portfolio-section__head"><h2>The Decision: Rebuild It</h2></div>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        The ambition began as a childhood wish; the discipline that followed is the real
        contribution. A reconstruction of a vanished place is a claim about what was there, and the
        only way to make that claim trustworthy is to make every block answer to a source.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head"><h2>Before a Block, a Source</h2></div>
    <div class="archive-grid archive-grid--detailed">
      <article>
        <b>Official record</b><p>
          Expo guides, yearbooks, pavilion descriptions, press material and planning documents.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Spatial record</b><p>
          Historical maps, sector plans, aerial photography, satellite imagery and coordinates.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Visual record</b><p>
          Visitor photographs, construction images, broadcast footage and surviving web pages.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Living record</b><p>
          Existing buildings, post-Expo reuse, site visits and comparisons with the current city.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Community record</b><p>
          Fan archives, personal recollections and material scattered across discontinued platforms.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <b>Recovered web</b><p>
          Internet Archive captures and mirrored pages used when original Expo resources disappear.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--dark">
    <div class="portfolio-section__inner">
      <div class="portfolio-section__head">
        <h2>Reconstruction Is a Chain of Translation</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="expo-workflow">
        <span>Find</span><span>Verify</span><span>Locate</span><span>Measure</span><span>Model</span
        ><span>Convert</span><span>Build</span><span>Compare</span><span>Revise</span>
      </div>
      <div class="toolchain-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Google Earth</h3><p>
            Location, footprint, orientation, surviving traces and terrain context.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Plans & photographs</h3><p>
            Façade rhythm, openings, structural cues, colour, material and relative scale.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>SketchUp / Blender</h3><p>
            Intermediate geometric studies where direct block construction is inefficient.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>ObjToSchematic</h3><p>
            Reference conversion before manual correction in Minecraft's block grammar.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Minecraft</h3><p>
            The final reconstruction environment, navigable archive and shared building space.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section">
    <div class="portfolio-section__head">
      <h2>The Archive Has Three Horizons</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="method-architecture method-architecture--light">
      <article>
        <small>Stage I</small><h3>Reconstruct the site</h3><p>
          Research the original park, restore its map and rebuild key buildings and public space
          inside Minecraft.
        </p><b>Spatial foundation</b>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Stage II</small><h3>Deepen the record</h3><p>
          Continue locating plans and images, revise proportions and complete sector-level ground
          and building information.
        </p><b>Iterative archive</b>
      </article>
      <article>
        <small>Stage III</small><h3>Make it inhabitable</h3><p>
          Develop interpretation, exploration and participatory building on top of the reconstructed
          scene.
        </p><b>Public experience</b>
      </article>
    </div>
    <h3 class="portfolio-subhead">Project plan</h3>
    <div class="expo-plan">
      <article class="expo-plan__step expo-plan__step--active">
        <small>STEP 1 · IN PROGRESS</small>
        <h4>Build the Expo Park</h4>
        <p>
          Build the entire 2010 Shanghai Expo Park in Minecraft. Most of Sector A, the Expo Axis,
          the Expo Culture Center and the Theme Pavilion in Sector B are built to date.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="expo-plan__step">
        <small>STEP 2</small>
        <h4>Redesign the exhibits</h4>
        <p>Redesign and rebuild the exhibition content.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="expo-plan__step">
        <small>STEP 3</small>
        <h4>Make it playable</h4>
        <p>Design and develop games or interactive products based on the project.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="expo-plan__step">
        <small>FURTHER</small>
        <h4>Beyond 2010</h4>
        <p>Rebuild the representative buildings of past World Expos—EXPO 1970 and beyond.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="site-facts">
      <div><strong>5.29</strong><span>km² original Expo site</span></div>
      <div><strong>A–E</strong><span>five principal sectors</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>banks of the Huangpu River</span></div>
      <div><strong>Most</strong><span>visited world exposition in history</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
      The site map is more than navigation. It is the index that connects a building's coordinate,
      sector, country or function, evidence package, reconstruction status and revision history.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="portfolio-section portfolio-section--closing expo-closing">
    <div>
      <div class="portfolio-section__label">What this is, and what it isn't</div>
      <p class="portfolio-statement">
        What survives is a walkable reconstruction—a coherent, navigable place that restores the
        relationships isolated photographs cannot show—plus a building catalogue, a learning
        environment, and a participatory project open to correction.
      </p>
      <p class="portfolio-copy portfolio-copy--flush">
        The honesty cuts both ways: a new photograph can correct a window; a recovered plan can move
        a whole façade. Completion is less important than keeping every reconstruction open to
        evidence.
      </p>
      <div class="expo-support">
        <p>
          ExpoCraft is a third-party project supported purely by personal interest. If you can
          provide relevant information or want to join the project team, get in touch through the
          ExpoCraft site. If you would like to support us financially—by sponsoring a cup of
          coffee—there is an AFDIAN page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
